## 1. Introduction
Due to the unique structures and fascinating properties, such as tunable and extraordinary porosity, high mechanical stability, long-distance internal energy migration, high surface areas and catalytic activity [1,2], MOFs used in gas storage [3], chemical separation [4,5], drug delivery [6], and catalysis [7] have received extensive attentions in recent years. In addition to these attractive applications, MOFs have been also considered to be one of leading candidates for sensing materials, and various MOFs have been synthesized as sensing platforms for specific analytes such as $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{\MeHg^{+}}$ , TNT, $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ , etc. [8–13]. Among those research works, alteration of MOFs fluorescence, quenching or enhancement, were easily measured, which laid the foundation of sensing. Hererin, we were interested in developing novel MOFs-based chemical sensor strategy. Intriguingly, Hupp and co-workers have recently reported there was energy transfer from CdSe/ZnS core/shell QDs to porphyrin-based MOFs [14], which inspired us to investigate the more interactions between QDs and MOFs, further, design effective sensing platform.
It is well-known that anions usually play an important role in a wide range of chemical and biological processes, as well as in environment pollution, thus, the development of sensitive and selective sensing platform for detecting anions has been the subject of intensive research [15–19]. Phosphate (Pi) is a well-known contaminant of ground and surface water, and excess Pi usually leads to excessive algal growth followed by decomposition, depletion in dissolved oxygen, eutrophication and a reduction in water quality [20,21], therefore, the Pi detection is of great significance for the controlling and guarding against the eutrophication occurrence. Up to now, a lot of methods have been developed for the quantitative analysis of Pi, including colorimetry [22,23], fluorometry [24–26], chromatography [27], electrochemical method [28,29] and enzymatic biosensors [30]. However, detection of Pi anions in an aqueous medium is influenced by the competing solvation effect [31], therefore, it is still a challenge for development of sensitive and selective method for the Pi determination. Herein, a novel fluorescent sensing platform using MOFs combined with ZnO QDs for the detection of Pi was reported. We  first demonstrated that MOFs could efficiently quench the fluorescence emission of ZnO
QDs and Pi ions inhibited the quenching effect, which resulted in the restoration of ZnO QDs fluorescence.
The proposed fluorescent sensing platform for phosphate was simply illustrated as Scheme 1, the positively charged ZnO QDs capped by (3-aminopropyl) trimethoxysilane (APTMS–ZnO QDs) were attached onto the negatively charged MOFs via the amine–Zn interaction and electrostatic interaction. The interactions resulted in the formation of flower-like complex, and aroused the quenching of ZnO QDs fluorescence through electron-transfer processes. However, when Pi was introduced into the system, the burst of Pi ions would inhibit the quenching effect immediately due to structure collapsing of MOFs, and recover the fluorescence of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs. In addition, the fluorescence intensity was intimately related to the concentrations of Pi ions and scarcely affected by other metal ions, anions and small molecules. Therefore, a novel fluorescent sensing platform for highly sensitive and selective detection of Pi has been successfully established.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
All the reagents were analytical grade and used without further purification. Potassium hydroxide (KOH), Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ , sodium phosphate $(\mathsf{N a}_{3}\mathsf{P O}_{4})$ , ethanol absolute and ethyl acetate were obtained from Chengdu Kelong Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Chengdu, China). Zinc acetate dihydrate $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Zn}}(\boldsymbol{0}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{c})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0})$ and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) were obtained from Tianjin Kermel Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Tianjin, China). Dichloromethane $\mathrm{(CH_{2}C l_{2})}$ ) was purchased from Dengke Reagent Co. Ltd. (Tianjin, China). (3-Aminopropyl) trimethoxysilane (APTMS) and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry Co. Ltd. Ultrapure water $(18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\mathrm{cm})$ obtained from a water purification system (ULUPURE, Chengdu, China) was used throughout this work.
## 2.2. Instrumentations
All fluorescence measurements were obtained with an F-7000 fluorescence spectrophotometer (Hitachi Co., Tokyo, Japan). The UV–vis absorption spectra were recorded by a U-2910 UV/Vis spectrophotometer (Hitachi Co., Tokyo, Japan). The SEM images were recorded on a JEOL JSM-7500F scanning electron microscope at $30.0\mathrm{kV}$ . The powder X-ray diffraction patterns (XRD) were recorded with an $\mathbf{\nabla}_{X}^{}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\nabla}_{}$ Pert Pro X-ray diffractometer using Co $\upkappa\upalpha$ irradiation $(\lambda=1.78897\mathring{\mathrm{A}}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ), with a $2\theta$ range from $5^{\circ}$ to $60^{\circ}$ at a scanning rate of $4^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ (Philips, Netherlands). The fluorescence lifetime was measured with a FL-TCSPC fluorescence spectrophotometer (Horiba Jobin Yvon Inc, France), and the NanoLED-370 was used as excitation source (Peak wavelength: $375\mathrm{nm}$ ; Pulse duration: 1.1 ns). The transmission electron microscope (TEM) image of APTES-capped ZnO QDs was obtained from a Tecnai $G^{2}$ F20 STWIN transmission electron microscope at an accelerating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}$ (FEI Co., America).
## 2.3. Preparation of APTMS-capped ZnO QDs
The synthesis of APTMS-capped ZnO QDs followed a two-step procedure, which was described in the previous literature [32]. In the first step, $3.0\mathrm{mM}$ of KOH was dissolved in $\mathrm{8\mL}$ of ethanol and kept in an ultrasonic bath for $40\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. The obtained KOH solution was dropwise added to $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}$ ethanol solution (0.01 M, $100\mathrm{mL}$ ) followed by continuous stirring for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The ZnO QDs were precipitated by addition of ethyl acetate. After centrifugation, the precipitate was washed by absolute ethanol for three times to remove un-reacted materials, and the final particles dispersed in ethanol $(20\mathrm{mL})$ .
In the second step, the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs were capped with APTMS by adding $0.05\mathsf{M}$ APTMS ethanol solution $(10\mathrm{mL})$ to the as prepared 20 mL  ZnO QDs solution dispersed in ethanol, then $0.40\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water was quickly added under vigorous stirring. The reaction mixture was stirred for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . The product was obtained by ultracentrifugation, and washed with absolute ethanol for three times and finally dispersed in deionized water.
## 2.4. Synthesis of metal–organic frameworks (MOF-5s)
The MOF-5 was prepared approximately according to the published literatures with some modifications [33,34]. In brief, $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ $(0.596\mathrm{g},\ 2.0\mathrm{mM})$ and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{I_{2}B D C)\ (0.0664g,\ 0.4m M)}$ were dissolved in $60\mathrm{mL}$ N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF) by stirring until complete dissolution of the solids. And then, the mixture was transferred into a $100\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave, which was sealed and maintained at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $21\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the obtained products were washed with DMF and immersed in fresh dichloromethane $(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2})$ for 2 days. As-prepared crystals were dried in vacuum at $40^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain MOF-5. Finally, 10 mg MOF-5 crystals were dispersed in $10\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water and kept in an ultrasonic bath for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature. The dispersive MOF-5 solution was stored at $5^{\circ}C$ and used for following experiments.
## 2.5. Fluorescence assay of phosphate
$25\upmu\mathrm{L}$ APTMS–ZnO QDs solution and $30\upmu\mathrm{L}$ MOFs dispersion solution $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{'}1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ ) were added to a $1.5\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tube. Then, different concentrations of Pi solution were quickly added, the mixture was diluted into $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ with deionized water and mixed thoroughly. After incubating for $40\mathrm{min}$ , the fluorescence spectra of the resulting solution were recorded by excitation with $337\mathrm{nm}$ light, during which the spectral bandwidths were kept at $5.0\mathrm{nm}$ . Fluorescence intensity was measured at $540\mathrm{nm}$ . During the sample measurement, the tap water samples were collected from Sichuan University campus.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of APTMS–ZnO QDs and MOFs
APTMS–ZnO QDs and MOF-5 were employed as the fluorophore and quencher in the present methodology, respectively. Fig. 1a shows their UV–vis absorption and fluorescence emission spectra. The characteristic absorption peak of ZnO QDs was located at $340\mathrm{nm}$ , and the aqueous suspensions of MOF-5 displayed a maximum absorption peak at $238\mathrm{nm}$ . Upon excitation with a $337\mathrm{nm}$ beam, the fluorescence spectrum of the ZnO QDs showed a maximum  emission at $540\mathrm{nm}$ , while no peak was observed for MOFs.
Fig. 1b shows the typical TEM image of as-prepared APTMS–ZnO QDs, revealing that these APTMS–ZnO QDs were spherical with a diameter of $5\mathrm{nm}$ , approximately. The original morphologies of MOF-5 (Fig. 1c) were well-shaped cubic crystals in the range of $40{-}80\upmu\mathrm{m}$ width. Furthermore, after dispersing in water by ultrasonic stripping, the MOF-5 turned to rhombic crystals of $2.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in width (Fig. 1d), which indicated that ultrasound could reduce the crystalline size of MOF-5. On the basis of the SEM images, the process of ultrasonic stripping was illustrated as Scheme 2.
## 3.2. ZnO QDs fluorescence quenching by MOFs
As shown in Fig. 2a, the addition of MOFs to the aqueous solution of APTMS–ZnO QDs would led to a significant quenching $(\sim70\%)$ of the fluorescence of QDs without obvious shift of the fluorescence emission peak. In contrast, Fig. 2b indicated that the interaction between MOFs and bare ZnO QDs (without capped by APTMS) aroused little fluorescence quenching $(\sim10\%)$ . The further experiment showed that the quenching efficiency would be enhanced obviously after ZnO QDs were capped by APTMS. In the other words, the APTMS–ZnO QDs would bring in a proximity to MOFs surface through the amine–Zn interaction between the APTMS and the zinc atoms in MOFs [14]. The results of zeta potential confirmed the existence of electrostatic interaction between $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs and MOFs (Fig. 2c), and it also revealed that $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs were positively charged and MOFs were negatively charged under the present experimental conditions. During the interacting with MOFs, there would be a significant decrease of the zeta potential from $24.2\mathrm{mV}$ of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs to $11.2\mathrm{mV}$ of complex. It turned out that the interactions between ZnO QDs and MOFs led to the fluorescence quenching of QDs. Fig. 2d shows the fluorescence quenching of ZnO QDs at various volume of MOFs $(1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.\$ , and we can find that the fluorescence intensity decreased along with the volume of MOFs increases. Furthermore, over $90\%$ of the fluorescence would be quenched during the addition of $40\upmu\mathrm{L}$ MOFs solution (inset of Fig. 2d). Chen and co-workers have claimed that Cu $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{dtoa})$ could quench the fluorescence of dye, where ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ in Cu ( $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{dtoa})$ played an important role in the fluorescence quenching [35]. In contrast, the presence of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ did not result in fluorescence quenching of ZnO QDs (Fig. 3a), which largely supported the coordination centers $(Z\mathsf{n}^{2+})$ of MOF-5 were not the main factors of fluorescence quenching in the present system. Thus, in order to reveal the mechanism of fluorescence quenching, timeresolved fluorescence spectra of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs and the QDs–MOFs complex were explored in detail (Fig. 3b, Table 1). It can be found that the fluorescence lifetime of ZnO QDs decreased after interacted with MOFs, and the reduced fluorescence lifetime implied that the quenching of ZnO QDs by MOFs was more likely to be a dynamic quenching process [36]. Therefore, the mechanism of fluorescence quenching might involve an electron transfer from ZnO QDs to MOFs.
3.3. Detection of Pi based on the inhibited quenching of ZnO QDs fluorescence
The feasibility and performance of the ZnO QDs–MOFs fluorescence sensing platform for the detection of Pi was also investigated in detail. As shown in Fig. 3c, in the presence of Pi, the quenching of the fluorescence of the ZnO QDs was obviously eliminated, and the fluorescence intensity was proportional to the concentration of Pi. Meanwhile, the inhibition effect on fluorescence quenching of ZnO QDs could be visual representation in the inset of Fig. 3c. Experiments were carried out to exclude the possibility of direct quenching of ZnO QDs fluorescence by Pi, the results showed that Pi has a negligible effect on fluorescence intensity of QDs at a comparable concentration (Fig. 3d).
## 3.4. Mechanism of the fluorescent sensing platform
The mechanism of this sensing platform was explored through a series of experiments. As shown in Fig. 4, the UV–vis absorption spectrum of QDs–MOFs complex showed characteristic band at $238\mathrm{nm}$ for MOFs but no absorption peak for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs, which attributed to the encapsulation of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs by MOFs. However, addition of Pi leading to an obvious characteristic band at $340\mathrm{nm}$ for ZnO QDs and a significant intensity decreasing of MOFs. It is assumed that Pi damaged the structure of MOFs, releasing free QDs from the ZnO QDs–MOFs complex.
In order to identify the formation of the QDs–MOFs complex and the collapse of the MOFs in the presence of Pi, SEM was performed. As shown in Fig. 5a, the original morphologies of MOFs were regular rhombus crystals, however, when ZnO QDs were added, the interactions between $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs and MOFs would led to the formation of flower-like QDs–MOFs complex, and the diameter of flower-like crystal was proximate ca. $1.0{-}2.0\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 5b). However, as shown in Fig. 5c, the formed complex would be aggregated with the addition of Pi, in the meantime, the MOFs began to collapse, which was consistent with the control experiment results that Pi would induced aggregation of MOFs and damaged their structure because of the coordination of zinc atoms in MOFs with Pi [37] (Fig. 5d).
The phenomenon was also evidenced by XRD analysis (Fig. 6). The major sharp diffraction peaks at $2\theta=6.9^{\circ}$ and $9.7^{\circ}$ were attributed to the (2 0 0) and (2 2 0) reflections of MOF-5, respectively, which were the feature of this material structure and were similar to the data published in previous literature for MOF-5 [38]. After interacting with ZnO QDs, some major peaks of MOFs disappeared and the other peaks were in disorder, which suggested the change of the crystal structure of MOFs, on the other hand, the emergence of ZnO QDs diffraction peaks indicated the formation of flower-like MOFs-QDs complex. Moreover, the introduction of Pi inhibited the interactions between QDs and MOFs and further damaged the structure of MOFs, which released ZnO QDs and recovered the fluorescence.
## 3.5. Calibration and sensitivity
This sensing platform proved good sensitivity for detection of Pi, as shown in Fig. 7a, good linear relationship was found between the relative fluorescence intensity and the concentrations of Pi in the range from $0.5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $12\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The calibration function for Pi analysis was represented with $\Delta F/F_{0}=0.09964+0.04616\mathsf C$ ( $R=0.9919\AA$ , where C was the concentration of Pi $(\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , $F_{0}$ was the fluorescence intensity of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs at $540\mathrm{nm}$ and $\Delta F{=}F{-}F_{\mathrm{q}}$ (F and $F_{\mathbb{q}}$ were the fluorescence intensity of QDs–MOFs complex in the presence and absence of Pi, respectively). And the detection limit was calculated to be $53\mathrm{nM}$ $(S/\mathbb{N}=3)$ ). Compared to these developed phosphate detection methods, the sensitivity of this fluorescent sensing platform was quite competitive as shown in Table 2.
## 3.6. Selectivity of the fluorescent sensing platform
Except for the requirement of sensitivity, good specificity was also needed. To evaluate the specificity of the developed fluorescent sensing platform for Pi, a series of relevant anions, metal ions and amino acids were studied as interference analytes. The results were shown in Fig. 7b, several anions, including ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , $S0_{3}{}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{C l^{-}}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf O_{4}{}^{2-}$ , ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ , exhibited almost no influence on Pi detection. Typical metal ions such as ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ , $A|^{3+}$ , $C a^{2+}$ ,
$Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{A}}{\mathsf{g}}^{+}$ , and amino acids such as leucine (Leu), histidine (His), arginine (Arg), glutamate (Glu), serine (Ser), cysteine (Cys), ascorbic acid (AA), alanine (Ala) also showed minimal interference in Pi determination. The results demonstrated that the present fluorescent sensing platform had highly selectivity to Pi due to the specific interactions between MOFs and Pi.
## 3.7. Determination of phosphate in tap water
To validate the reliability of the proposed fluorescent sensing platform in practical applications, the detection of Pi in tap water was carried out by standard addition method. The results given in Table 3 show that the recoveries of spiked samples ranged from $95\%$ to $104\%$ with relative standard deviation (RSD) of around $2\%$ . Moreover, there was negligible fluorescence background at $540\mathrm{nm}$ under excitation of $337\mathrm{nm}$ in water samples, which confirmed that the presented fluorescent method was feasible for the detection of Pi in water samples.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, we have demonstrated that MOFs acted as ultrahighly efficient quencher for APTMS-capped ZnO QDs, moreover, the coexisting of Pi can inhibit the quenching effect and result in the restoration of ZnO QDs fluorescence. Based on this phenomenon, a novel fluorescent sensing platform for highly sensitive and selective detection of Pi has been successfully developed. This work offered a pathway for utilizing MOFs to quench the fluorescence of QDs and paved the road for design and development of new fluorescent sensing platform based QDs–MOFs interactions, which expanded the applications of MOFs in the fields of chemical and biomedical sensing.
